{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg-2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Keeper of Gallow Point\n\nEvery evening for thirty-one years, Elspeth Marr had climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of the Gallow Point lighthouse, wound the great clockwork that turned the lens, and lit the lamp before the sun slipped behind the western cliffs. The routine had become a kind of prayer. Her hands knew it without her.\n\nThe island was small, a knuckle of granite a mile off the coast, and in winter it belonged to the wind. Her only company was the supply boat that came twice a month, the gulls that quarreled on the railings, and the sea, which never repeated itself and never stopped talking.\n\nHer husband, Tomas, had kept the light before her. When his heart gave out one February, the mainland office offered to send a relief keeper, a young man with a city accent and a sour stomach for boats. Elspeth had said no. She had said it before she had quite decided to, the way you pull your hand from a stove. The office, perhaps moved by her grief or perhaps tired of arguing, had let her stay.\n\nSome nights she regretted it. The loneliness sat beside her at the kitchen table and drank her tea with her. But most nights she was grateful, because the light was not only a machine. It was a promise. Somewhere out in the black water, a fisherman who could not see his own hand was watching for the beam, and it told him where the rocks were, and where home was.\n\nThat autumn, the logbook filled with ordinary entries. Wind southwest, force five. Visibility good. Lamp trimmed. Gull found dead on the north ledge, buried at low tide. The entries were the only letters she wrote anymore.\n\nOn the last night of October, a storm came up faster than the forecast had promised. By nine o'clock the glass was falling and the sea had turned the color of slate. Elspeth climbed the steps with a lantern in one hand and the rail in the other, her coat snapping around her. At the top, the lamp room shook. Rain struck the panes like thrown gravel.\n\nShe lit the lamp. The beam swept out across the water, and for a moment she saw nothing but foam. Then, low on the horizon, a single small light answered, bobbing and uncertain.\n\nIt was a fishing boat, and it was coming in wrong. Its engine was coughing. It was heading straight for the Teeth, the reef that had taken three boats in her time on the island.\n\nElspeth did not think. She grabbed the signal horn that Tomas had installed for fog and pulled the cord, long and short, long and short, the old warning. She swung the lamp's shutter so the beam flashed in the pattern the fishermen were taught as children. Then she did it again. And again.\n\nThe small light wavered. Slowly, painfully, it turned.\n\nShe kept signaling until her arms burned and her throat was raw. The storm hammered the tower, and the stairs groaned, and she did not stop. Near midnight the boat's light came around the eastern point and slid into the harbor's lee, where the water lay flat as glass.\n\nElspeth sank down against the iron wall of the lamp room and laughed, a little wildly, into the dark.\n\nIn the morning the storm had blown itself out. The sea lay quiet and bruised-blue. Two fishermen came up the steps at dawn, bringing a basket of bread and a bottle of brandy and no words that were adequate, and Elspeth made them tea.\n\nShe wrote in the logbook that day, as she always did. Wind northeast, force eight, easing. Lamp trimmed. One vessel guided to harbor.\n\nThen she sat for a long time with the pen in her hand, listening to the sea, and thought that perhaps Tomas had been right after all. The light was never really about the rock. It was about the people who needed to find their way home, and about the one who stayed awake to show them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_